<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lashio</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lashio is the largest town of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> in Myanmar. It was the starting point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> Road, built in 1937–1938 to carry supplies from the railhead at Lashio to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> in Nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; Japanese forces captured it in April 1942, cutting the route. It has a large Chinese community and is a centre of trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lashio.jpg" alt="Lashio" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: nico · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lashio_sight_IMG_0595.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lashio is the largest city and the capital of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a>, about 200 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. It stands on a low mountain spur overlooking the valley of the Yaw River, and Loi Leng, the highest mountain of the Shan Hills, lies 45 kilometres to the south-east. The population is mostly Shan, Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Bamar</a>. It grew from about 5,000 in 1960 to 88,590 in 1983 and was estimated at about 131,000 in 2009. It is the home of Lashio University and several other colleges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before British rule Lashio was the centre of authority for the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a>, but the Burmese post lay close to the Nam Yao, in an old Chinese fortified camp. The valley had been very populous until a rebellion started by the sawbwa of Hsenwi, about ten years before the British occupation, ruined it. British colonial rule in this part of the country began in 1887, and the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> reached Lashio in 1903. In 1900 the town had a European station with a court house, a military police post, a bazaar, and a native station where Shans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims lived in separate quarters, with reserves for the temporary residences of the five sawbwas of the northern Shan States.</p><p>Lashio became important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> as the Burmese terminus of the Burma Road. Japanese forces took it on 29 April 1942, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> liberated it on 7 March 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lashio lies at the end of the Burma Road and at the terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>–Lashio railway, and the Goteik viaduct, the highest bridge in Myanmar, is near it. It has a large Chinese population and since 2000 has been important for border trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Landmarks include the Yan Tine Aung Pagoda, the Mansu Shan Monastery and a Chinese temple that many visit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-new-year">Chinese New Year</a>.</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-1027">Operation 1027</a> of the Myanmar civil war, the Ta'ang National Liberation Army and other rebel forces took towns around Lashio. After the ceasefire collapsed, the Myanmar National Democratic Alliance Army and its allies attacked the city in the Battle of Lashio and took it in August 2024. A Chinese-brokered ceasefire was agreed on 20 January 2025, and in April 2025 officials of the State Administration Council returned to the city as the MNDAA withdrew, reinstating the council's administration by 21 April.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lashio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>North Hsenwi, with its capital at Hsenwi town, covered the north-eastern lands along the frontier with Yunnan, including <b>Lashio</b> and the Kutkai area.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-hsenwi">North Hsenwi</a></li></ul></section>
